"Notice of Respondent's Rights—CARE Act Proceedings | California Courts | Self Help Guide (https://www.selfhelp.courts.ca.gov/jcc-form/CARE-113)\nciteturn2391search0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 weeks ago; Get form CARE-113\n\n# Notice of Respondent's Rights—CARE Act Proceedings (CARE-113)\n\nTells respondents what their rights are in the CARE Act process.\n\nGet form CARE-113\n\n  * 汉语Get form CARE-113 in Chinese Simplified (Chinese Simplified)\n  * فارسیGet form CARE-113 in Farsi (Farsi)\n  * 한국어Get form CARE-113 in Korean (Korean)\n  * españolGet form CARE-113 in Spanish (Spanish)\n  * Tiếng ViệtGet form CARE-113 in Vietnamese (Vietnamese)\n\nEffective: September 1, 2024--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCARE Act forms | California Courts | Self Help Guide (https://selfhelp.courts.ca.gov/care-act/forms)\nciteturn2391search1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; If you are not sure which forms to use, talk to your self-help denter or a lawyer. ... Petition to Begin CARE Act Proceedings*  | CARE-100  | Use this form to ask a court to start CARE Act proceedings on behalf of someone. ... Notice of Respondent's Rights—CARE Act Proceedings*  | CARE-113  | Tells respondents what their rights are in the CARE Act process.\n\n# CARE Act forms\n\nAll California courts use the same basic set of forms for CARE Act proceedings. You can find the most commonly-used forms on this page. If you are not sure which forms to use, talk to your self-help denter or a lawyer.\n\nSome courts have special local forms too. To see if you will need any special local forms, contact your court clerk or check your court’s website (link opens in a new tab).\n\n🔗 All links to court forms below open in a new tab so you don´t lose your place.\n\nForm names marked with an asterisk (\"*\") are adopted for mandatory use by all courts.\n\nForm Name  | Form Number  | Purpose\n--- | --- | ---\nInformation for Petitioners—About the CARE Act   | CARE-050-INFO  | Explains the CARE Act process, provides information about petitioner and respondent eligibility, notes required documentation and gives instruction on how to properly fill out the petition, form CARE-100.\nInformation for Respondents—About the CARE Act  | CARE-060-INFO  | Provides information for the respondent about the CARE Act. Explains what CARE Act proceedings are, summarizes petitioner’s and respondent’s rights, and describes the role of a supporter.\nPetition to Begin CARE Act Proceedings*  | CARE-100  | Use this form to ask a court to start CARE Act proceedings on behalf of someone.\nMental Health Declaration – CARE Act Proceedings*  | CARE-101  | A licensed behavioral health professional uses this form to tell the court that they have determined that the respondent meets, or have reason to believe that the respondent meets, the legal requirements for CARE Act proceedings. You must file this form with a form CARE-100.\nPetition to Begin CARE Act Proceedings by Licensed Behavioral Health Professional Only*  | CARE-102  | A licensed behavioral health professional may use this form to ask a court to start CARE Act proceedings on behalf of someone. This form combines the information from forms CARE-100 and CARE-101, so the licensed behavioral health professional can use this form instead of submitting both a CARE-100 and CARE-101.\nOrder for CARE Act Report*  | CARE-105  | States the court’s decision on requiring a county agency to investigate and file a report that includes whether the respondent meets or is likely to meet the legal requirements for the CARE process.\nNotice of Order for CARE Act Report*  | CARE-106  | Provides notice that a court has ordered a county agency to investigate and file a report that includes whether the respondent meets or is likely to meet the legal requirements for the CARE process.\nProof of Personal Service of Notice of Order for CARE Act Report  | CARE-107  | Tells the court that the Notice of Order for CARE Act Report (form CARE-106) was delivered (served) in person to the respondent.\nNotice of Initial Appearance—CARE Act Proceedings*  | CARE-110  | Provides notice of the first court appearance to the respondent, the respondent’s counsel, and the county behavioral health agency in the county where the respondent lives (if different from where the proceedings have started). Also, if the county behavioral health agency is not the petitioner, to provide notice to the petitioner and county behavioral health agency in the county where the proceedings have started (if different from where the respondent lives).\nProof of Personal Service of Notice of Initial Appearance—CARE Act Proceedings  | CARE-111  | Tells the court that the Notice of Initial Appearance—CARE Act Proceedings (form CARE-110) has been delivered (served) in person to the respondent.\nNotice of Respondent's Rights—CARE Act Proceedings*  | CARE-113  | Tells respondents what their rights are in the CARE Act process.\nNotice of Hearing—CARE Act Proceedings*  | CARE-115  | Gives notice of any CARE Act hearing that occurs after the first court appearance.\nProof of Personal Service of Notice of Hearing—CARE Act Proceedings  | CARE-116  | Tells the court that the Notice of Hearing (form CARE-115) has been delivered (served) in person to the respondent.\nNotice of Hearing or Continuance  | CARE-118  | Gives notice to certain original CARE Act petitioners that a hearing is being held.\nNotice of Dismissal  | CARE-119  | Gives notice to certain original CARE Act petitioners that the case which they filed a petition for has been dismissed.\nRequest for New Order and Hearing—CARE Act Proceedings  | CARE-120  | Asks the judge for a new or changed order and/or a hearing based on that request at any time during the CARE process.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n| California Courts | Self Help Guide (https://selfhelp.courts.ca.gov/find-forms/all?page=1)\nciteturn2391search2 [wordlim: 200] Cr